 machine-check sink coverage at the module handoff

F2 fix (PR #637 audit): emit_cmp_zero_mem_sized completed its line with a
raw buf push and no debug_scan_tail() - the one sink (verified by a
mechanical sweep over all 38 AsmOutput methods) that falsified the
"every public emit method ends in debug_scan_tail()" banner. Benign
today (a compare writes flags only), but precisely the escape hatch the
epoch validator exists to close: a future %rax-writing form of that
method would have been invisible to the validator. The method now scans.

Going one step further than fixing the cited sink: sink coverage is now
MACHINE-CHECKED instead of promised. debug_scan_tail classifies every
emitted-but-unclassified complete line from a scanned_len watermark (the
old tail-only form silently lost lines when an emit carried an embedded
newline, and re-counted nothing after the exploratory dual-layout
truncation rewound the buffer - both now handled: the watermark rewinds
with the truncation, and every complete line is classified exactly
once). At the single module handoff in generate_module_with_debug,
debug_assert_fully_scanned() asserts scanned_len == buf.len(): any
future emit method that completes a line without a scan fails CI's
assertions-ON corpus step naming its first offending line, instead of
silently blinding the validator. Foreign-ISA targets return immediately
(they never scan).

Also fixed en route: the acc-epoch assertion's "Last unclassified
emitted line" context always printed None - the AsmOutput field was
declared but never written. The classifier now records the actual line
text in a thread-local at the point of classification, and
debug_last_unclassified surfaces it (owned return; the field is gone).

Tests pin: cmp_zero_mem scans and moves no epoch; a raw unscanned push
fails the handoff assert and is classified exactly once by the next
scan; embedded-newline emits classify every line (pushq+popq = one
bump); the diagnostic record carries the actual line. Full suite with
debug-assertions active: 3554/3554 - the handoff assert runs inside
every module-level codegen test with zero trips, proving no second sink
lurks.
