From 1c085a00d93ebacd151e8b1aae152beae0f6ebaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:30:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] test: serialize the backend tri-state flag windows and
 restore the true previous state

F3 fix (PR #637 audit): the flag flip tests
(set_commuted_acc_for_tests / set_hidden_read_copy_chain_for_tests)
flipped a process-global atomic by hand and restored a FORCED state:
set(false); run; set(true) leaked "forced ON" past any assertion
failure between the pair (nothing restores on panic), and even on the
success path the restore target was wrong - the tri-state contract is
-1 = env-follow, so a suite run with CCC_NO_COMMUTED_ACC or
CCC_HIDDEN_READ_COPY_CHAIN set produced outcomes that depended on test
order. Nothing serialized the windows either: cargo runs tests on a
shared thread pool, so a flip window was concurrent with every other
test reading the flag.

This adopts the repo's existing test_support discipline (the module
exists precisely because hand-restored shared state leaks under the
pool) for the backend flags:

- test_support::BACKEND_FLAG_LOCK: one process-wide lock for both
  override atomics (module-local locks would serialize each module
  against itself and nothing else), with ENV_WINDOW_DEPTH-style
  reentrancy (a precise-mode pin held across asserts while a
  conservative window scopes a second compute is a real shape).
- test_support::TriStateFlagWindow: sets the atomic on acquire, restores
  the PREVIOUS value on drop - panic-safe, correct across env contexts,
  holding the lock for its whole lifetime so flip windows are mutually
  exclusive.
- The three flip tests use windows; the flag-shaped default-mode tests
  (commuted-RHS eligibility, narrow-width M2 coverage, precise
  hidden-read walk) pin the mode they assert (forced-commuted /
  forced-precise) so their outcome no longer depends on the environment
  or on scheduling. The hand-rolled bool setters are gone.

Acceptance: cargo test -- --test-threads=8 green repeatedly in ALL FOUR
env contexts (knobs unset, NO_COMMUTED_ACC set, HIDDEN_READ_COPY_CHAIN
set, both set) - previously the knob-set runs were order-dependent;
the bisection knobs keep working for real compilations.
